1
00:00:39,890 --> 00:00:42,640
Haha haha! Yeah! Whoo!

2
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:47,360
Ahh, What a nice way to spend our last day going to Adelaide's house.

3
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:48,860
Our journey is finally over.

4
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:50,420
Pretty soon I'll be back home.

5
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:53,600
I won't have to worry about that woodsman or that beast or whatever,

6
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:55,860
and you won't have to put up with us anymore.

7
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:57,450
Bet you're pretty happy, huh, Beatrice?

8
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:58,210
Huh?

9
00:00:58,210 --> 00:01:00,450
Oh... Yeah. Sure.

10
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:01,450
You all right?

11
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:04,370
You sound uncharacteristically... wistful.

12
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:05,200
What?

13
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,870
Sorry. Just thinking.

14
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:07,880
Hmm.

15
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:09,860
♪ Oh, we're going to the pasture ♪

16
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:11,380
♪ To meet Adelaide and ask her ♪

17
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:15,300
♪ if she has a way to send us back where we came from ♪

18
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:16,610
♪ I don't know who she is ♪

19
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:17,450
♪ or how she is, ♪

20
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:18,770
♪ or when, what, why she is ♪

21
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:20,040
- ♪ But as for where she is, ♪
- ♪ ...but as for where she is, ♪

22
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:21,720
- ♪ she is where we will go. ♪
- ♪ she is where we will go. ♪

23
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,450
- ♪ To Adelaide! ♪
- ♪ To Adelaide! ♪

24
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:25,480
- ♪ Adelaide! ♪
- ♪ Adelaide! ♪

25
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:26,480
- ♪ Come on and join ♪
- ♪ Come on and join ♪

26
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:28,480
- ♪ the Adelaide parade ♪
- ♪ the Adelaide parade ♪

27
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:30,480
- ♪ Adelaide! ♪
- ♪ Adelaide! ♪

28
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:31,570
- ♪ To Adelaide! ♪
- ♪ To Adelaide! ♪

29
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,320
- ♪ we're going to ♪
- ♪ we're going to ♪

30
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:35,480
- ♪ Adelaide's house today ♪
- ♪ Adelaide's house today ♪

31
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,740
Uh, haha,  ha, ha, ha, hehe !

32
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:41,240
Boy. Finally going home.

33
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:42,790
huuh...

34
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:44,790
Hey, what's with Beatrice?

35
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:45,530
I don't know.

36
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:47,960
What's with George Washington being naked?

37
00:01:49,630 --> 00:01:50,290
What?

38
00:01:50,290 --> 00:01:52,550
All the other frogs are dressed up nice.

39
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:53,550
uhWoah!

40
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:54,800
Uhwoah.

41
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:56,130
He's an outcast,

42
00:01:56,130 --> 00:01:58,720
and he's cold.

43
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:00,800
Feel these cold feets.

44
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:01,800
No.

45
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,270
He's supposed to be
cold, Greg. He's a frog.

46
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:06,020
But he's our frog!

47
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:07,020
Well, he's not my frog!

48
00:02:07,230 --> 00:02:08,700
or she's not my frog.

49
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:09,940
We don't really know its gender.

50
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:11,940
Huh! Come on, George.

51
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:12,940
You're a manly frog,

52
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:14,640
and you need some socks.

53
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:15,940
Graawr

54
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:17,490
Fweeew!

55
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,660
Uh-oh. It looks like we're in trouble.

56
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:22,740
Huh!
It's 'cause the president is nude!

57
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,930
It's probably 'cause you snuck on without paying.

58
00:02:24,930 --> 00:02:25,470
Oh, yeah.

59
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:27,410
B-But that's because Greg threw all our money away.

60
00:02:27,410 --> 00:02:28,630
Take him, not me!

61
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:33,330
Sorry!

62
00:02:41,190 --> 00:02:42,530
Tadpoles!

63
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:44,010
Your babies!

64
00:03:01,030 --> 00:03:02,440
Good day! Gentle...

65
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:03,130
Ahem...

66
00:03:03,130 --> 00:03:04,450
Good day, gentlemen.

67
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:05,030
We-

68
00:03:05,030 --> 00:03:07,040
I-I must be going now to join the band

69
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:10,870
Wirt, drum me!

70
00:03:10,870 --> 00:03:11,480
Okay.

71
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,210
Ow! Ow, ow!
Ow! Ow, ow!

72
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:17,480
Are we good?

73
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:19,010
Whew! I think so.

74
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:21,160
Wirt, sometimes you got to face your problems.

75
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:22,010
Turn yourself in

76
00:03:22,010 --> 00:03:23,300
and get kicked off this boat.

77
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,010
I don't think today's a good day to get arrested by frogs.

78
00:03:26,010 --> 00:03:27,440
Hmm, well...

79
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:28,770
Hey! What's going on?

80
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:30,770
Don't push me!

81
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:36,070
Okay, everybody.

82
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:36,980
Act natural.

83
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:37,980
Drum me! Drum me!

84
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:39,280
I said, "act natural."

85
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,150
It is natural! I'm a drum!

86
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:47,910
Wirt, drum me!

87
00:03:47,910 --> 00:03:49,080
Drum me in the face!

88
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:50,610
Okay, okay! Shh!

89
00:03:50,610 --> 00:03:52,670
Don't draw attention to us!

90
00:03:52,670 --> 00:03:55,380
Whoa!

91
00:03:58,640 --> 00:03:59,920
Woah!

92
00:04:01,090 --> 00:04:02,640
No bassoon player.

93
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:03,510
Uh-oh.

94
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:09,100
Dog, those frogs
really love the bassoon!

95
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:11,640
Huh?

96
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:13,140
Hmm.

97
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:13,900
Oh, no.

98
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:16,150
Beatrice, I'm too young to go to frog jail.

99
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:18,270
Hey, why don't you play the bassoon?

100
00:04:18,270 --> 00:04:19,900
That'll get us kicked
off this boat for sure.

101
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:20,640
Huh!

102
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:22,950
No, Greg's right. You should play it.

103
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:24,210
Go ahead. You'll do fine.

104
00:04:24,210 --> 00:04:25,680
You play instruments, right?

105
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,600
Yeah, but bassoon and clarinet are way different.

106
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:29,450
I don't have the embouchure for bassoon.

107
00:04:29,450 --> 00:04:32,040
I mean, the lower and middle ranges have some similarities in terms of-

108
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:33,210
Wirt, you can do it.

109
00:04:33,210 --> 00:04:35,370
Seriously, nobody wants to hear me play.

110
00:04:35,370 --> 00:04:36,210
I do!

111
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:38,040
I do!

112
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:39,210
Graawr

113
00:04:40,450 --> 00:04:41,710
Uh...

114
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:45,130
Wirt?

115
00:04:45,130 --> 00:04:47,130
Yeah. H-Here we go.

116
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,230
Ow. Ow. Ow. Ow.

117
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:01,230
♪ At night when
the lake is a mirror ♪

118
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:02,150
Huh?

119
00:05:02,150 --> 00:05:02,630
Woah!

120
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:06,490
♪ And the moon rides
the waves to the shore ♪

121
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:11,080
♪ a single soul sets his voice singing ♪

122
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:16,580
♪ content to be slightly forlorn ♪

123
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:19,840
♪ a song rises over the lilies ♪

124
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:20,930
♪ wah-ooh, wah- ♪

125
00:05:20,930 --> 00:05:23,920
♪ Ooh, ooh, oohooh ♪

126
00:05:20,930 --> 00:05:24,920
♪ sweeps high to
clear over the reeds ♪

127
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:30,100
♪ and over the bullrushes swaying ♪

128
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:33,430
♪ to pluck at a pair of heartstrings ♪

129
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:34,850
Hey, Beatrice,

130
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:36,740
thanks for supporting my bassooning.

131
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:38,310
Yeah. You're actually...

132
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:39,060
good.

133
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:40,050
The best part is

134
00:05:40,050 --> 00:05:41,890
we're still on track to get to Adelaide's.

135
00:05:41,890 --> 00:05:43,440
Yep, that's great.

136
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:44,760
You don't seem thrilled.

137
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:46,160
Well, I just ...

138
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:47,950
I don't want you to...

139
00:05:47,950 --> 00:05:49,120
never mind. Never mind.

140
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:52,200
♪ ...carries their reverie on ♪

141
00:05:57,620 --> 00:05:59,290
Shh!

142
00:05:59,970 --> 00:06:01,290
Uh...

143
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:07,010
♪ Over the treetops and mountains ♪

144
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:10,720
♪ over the blackened ravines ♪

145
00:06:11,150 --> 00:06:11,930
Ohh...

146
00:06:11,930 --> 00:06:16,560
♪ Then softly it falls
by a house near a stream ♪

147
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:21,060
♪ and over the garden wall ♪

148
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:24,480
♪ To thee ♪

149
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:30,240
Greg: Haah... I knew you were special.

150
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:41,450
What are they doing?

151
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:43,000
Looks like they're...

152
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:45,760
hibernating in the mud.

153
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:49,300
So, where's Adelaide's house? Is it close?

154
00:06:49,630 --> 00:06:51,050
It's... hmm.

155
00:06:51,050 --> 00:06:53,190
We should probably just go tomorrow, I think.

156
00:06:53,190 --> 00:06:55,060
We don't want to bother her too late, you know?

157
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:56,320
What are we supposed to do...

158
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:58,390
just... sit around in the mud with these frogs?

159
00:06:58,390 --> 00:06:59,020
Huh!

160
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:01,020
I call that mud over there!

161
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:03,020
Hahaha! Yeah!

162
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:06,190
So, then what?

163
00:07:06,190 --> 00:07:08,440
Well, then I went to go talk to Sara.

164
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:10,340
I mean, like really talk to her, you know...

165
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:11,860
put all my cards on the table, you know?

166
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:12,700
Yeah...

167
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:13,480
And then!

168
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:16,500
Jason Funderberker comes out of nowhere and whisks her away!

169
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:18,370
Ugh! Jason Funderberker...

170
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:19,370
that guy.

171
00:07:20,370 --> 00:07:21,370
Plus Greg was around.

172
00:07:21,370 --> 00:07:23,080
He would have embarrassed me even more.

173
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:25,630
Well, Wirt, it sounds like you're a real loser back home.

174
00:07:25,630 --> 00:07:27,050
Oh, thanks a lot.

175
00:07:27,050 --> 00:07:28,740
I mean compared to how you are here.

176
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:30,720
Here you're like a hero and stuff, right?

177
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:31,860
Am I?

178
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:33,300
Well, I don't know if I'd say, "hero," but...

179
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:36,220
Do you think that I could be a hero, too? Huh?

180
00:07:40,930 --> 00:07:43,900
You done good, Mr. President.
You done good.

181
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:45,980
If I was you, I wouldn't even want to go home.

182
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:48,730
Yeah, but I-I can't stay here forever.

183
00:07:48,730 --> 00:07:49,990
Why not?

184
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:51,310
Hmm? Why not?

185
00:07:51,310 --> 00:07:53,680
Because... because I-I can't just...

186
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:55,780
hmph, maybe it is better to stay here.

187
00:07:55,780 --> 00:07:57,520
Great. Then it's agreed. We're not going to Adelaide's.

188
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:58,790
Good night, Wirt.

189
00:07:58,790 --> 00:08:00,120
Oh, what?

190
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:01,660
Oh. Good night, Beatrice.

191
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:13,220
Beatrice?

192
00:08:14,010 --> 00:08:15,120
Greg, wake up!

193
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:15,720
Huh?

194
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:16,880
Beatrice took off!

195
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:17,270
What?

196
00:08:17,270 --> 00:08:18,010
Come on!

197
00:08:24,270 --> 00:08:25,100
Come on, Greg!

198
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:27,110
I'm coming.

199
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:41,160
Close the flue.

200
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:43,850
That fresh air does simply gruesome things

201
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:46,400
to my tender, delicate skin.

202
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:48,000
Adelaide, we need to talk.

203
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,420
Did you bring me what I asked for?

204
00:08:50,710 --> 00:08:53,120
I found two brothers lost in the woods,

205
00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:56,680
but I can't give them to you, Adelaide. They need to go home.

206
00:08:56,680 --> 00:08:57,140
Nonsense!

207
00:08:57,140 --> 00:08:59,510
I'll give them a wonderful home here.

208
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:01,040
That's what you said, but...

209
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:03,850
Can't you see I'm sick and helpless?

210
00:09:04,850 --> 00:09:05,850
Ah-choo!

211
00:09:06,090 --> 00:09:07,610
I'm all alone in the world.

212
00:09:07,610 --> 00:09:09,610
I want a child servant!

213
00:09:09,610 --> 00:09:12,490
Servant?! I thought you just wanted
some yard work done.

214
00:09:12,490 --> 00:09:16,260
Our arrangement was for you to bring me a child servant

215
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:19,740
and then I give you the scissors...

216
00:09:21,330 --> 00:09:24,330
to snip, snip, snip your family's wings away

217
00:09:24,330 --> 00:09:25,750
to make them human again.

218
00:09:25,750 --> 00:09:27,500
What if I became your servant?

219
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:31,000
Ha! I need a big, strong child!

220
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,340
You can turn me into
a human, can't you?

221
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:36,090
Oh, yes ...scissors!

222
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:39,220
Yeah, yeah. So, give me the scissors.

223
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:40,600
I'll go help my family.

224
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:41,600
Huh! Wirt!

225
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:44,020
Close the door! I'll catch my death of cold!

226
00:09:44,020 --> 00:09:45,350
What's going on?

227
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:46,850
You shouldn't be here.

228
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:48,020
Adelaide?

229
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:51,360
Welcome home, children.

230
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:52,940
- Huh?
- Huh? Ah!

231
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:55,190
Beatrice, wh-what...

232
00:09:55,190 --> 00:09:56,280
I...

233
00:09:56,280 --> 00:09:57,280
But I...

234
00:09:57,280 --> 00:09:59,200
I thought we were friends.

235
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:01,030
Now they're mine.

236
00:10:01,030 --> 00:10:03,400
And once I fill their heads with wool,

237
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:05,770
they'll become just like little sheep

238
00:10:05,770 --> 00:10:08,460
and follow my every command.

239
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:11,540
All along, you've been leading us to this crazy lady?!

240
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:13,350
I do as he commands.

241
00:10:13,350 --> 00:10:15,020
the voice of the night,

242
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:19,050
the beast of eternal darkness.

243
00:10:20,550 --> 00:10:21,500
What are you doing?!

244
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:22,180
Ohh!

245
00:10:23,350 --> 00:10:25,100
The night air is poisonous!

246
00:10:25,270 --> 00:10:26,520
Breathe it in, lady!

247
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:27,940
Wirt, Greg, let's go!

248
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:29,520
The deadly... air!

249
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:31,310
Whoa! Geez! She wasn't kidding!

250
00:10:31,310 --> 00:10:33,520
It's fresh! Ohhhh!

251
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:34,020
Huh?

252
00:10:34,360 --> 00:10:35,860
Wirt? Greg?

253
00:10:40,420 --> 00:10:42,030
Greg! Wirt!

254
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:45,120
It wasn't what it looked like! I was just...

255
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:46,750
please come back!

256
00:10:46,750 --> 00:10:48,120
Ohh.

257
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:52,880
Wirt,

258
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:54,460
what about Beatrice?

259
00:10:55,940 --> 00:10:57,800
I shouldn't have trusted anyone.

260
00:10:58,260 --> 00:10:59,630
- Graawr
- Huh?

261
00:11:01,390 --> 00:11:02,800
Benjamin Franklin!

262
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:04,810
Graawr

263
00:11:06,210 --> 00:11:07,640
Hurry it up, Greg.
